{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-73335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(t+1){e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-73335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(t-1){e}^{-t} \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=1.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(1)=-1\\cdot {e}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-73335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba351d9d68f96daf6ab0874814211a5db2aeeeaf8dd71dfa0859ee9f908430"} {"question" "q-73335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-73335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}"] ["math" "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "t=2,\\,v=-\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{e}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-73335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=1,\\,v=-\\frac{1}{e}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2}{2\\sqrt{t}} & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2 & <0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & <-2 \\\\ t & <{e}^{-2} \\\\ t & <\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba351d9d68f96daf6ab0874814211a5db2aeeeaf8dd71dfa0859ee9f908430"} {"question" "q-109289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})"] ["math" "(0,\\frac{2}{e})"] ["math" "(1,e)"] ["math" "(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}},\\infty )"] ["math" "(e,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=t{e}^{-t},$ where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =(1-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-t} & <0 \\\\ 1-t & <0 \\\\ t & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (1,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba351d9d68f96daf6ab0874814211a5db2aeeeaf8dd71dfa0859ee9f908430"} {"question" "q-109290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,1)"] ["math" "(0,\\frac{1}{e})"] ["math" "(1,\\infty )"] ["math" "(1,e)"] ["math" "(e,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =14t-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(14t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =14-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14-6t & =0 \\\\ -6t & =-14 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{7}{3}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{7}{3}) & =14(\\frac{7}{3})-3{(\\frac{7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{3}-\\frac{49}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba351d9d68f96daf6ab0874814211a5db2aeeeaf8dd71dfa0859ee9f908430"} {"question" "q-109307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=2,\\,v=-4"] ["math" "t=2,\\,v=6"] ["math" "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{9}{2}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{15}{6}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{49}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-109307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{49}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-335924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-{t}^{2},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-335924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}-2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1-2t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a(t)=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{t} & =2 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2})+1-1 \\\\  & =ln\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=\\frac{1}{2},$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-335924" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9ba351d9d68f96daf6ab0874814211a5db2aeeeaf8dd71dfa0859ee9f908430"} {"question" "q-335924" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-335924" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133cef7e0b4fe1d8743c85f0ba7b55576a6a23bc22d694fc7dde9aeea"} {"question" "q-335924" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041130/state.json" "file_sha256" "be99941133c